{"name":"Tangerine Haze","hex":"#de8417","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"tangerine","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A slightly ovoid, orange-coloured  citrus fruit with a rough peel and a sour-sweet taste which is larger than a clementine and sometimes classed as a variety of mandarin orange.","A deep yellowish-orange colour, like that of a tangerine fruit."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a deep yellowish-orange colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/tangerine"},{"term":"haze","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Very fine solid particles (smoke, dust) or liquid droplets (moisture) suspended in the air, slightly limiting visibility.","A reduction of transparency of a clear gas or liquid."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To be or become hazy, or thick with haze."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/haze"}],"entries":[{"title":"Tangerine","blocks":[{"text":"The tangerine is a type of citrus fruit that is orange in colour, that is considered either a variety of the mandarin orange, or a closely related species, under the name Citrus tangerina, or yet as a hybrid of mandarin orange varieties, with some pomelo contribution."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2a/TangerineFruit.jpg/330px-TangerineFruit.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tangerine","wikidata":"Q516494"},{"title":"Haze","blocks":[{"text":"Haze is traditionally an atmospheric phenomenon in which dust, smoke, and other dry particulates suspended in air obscure visibility and the clarity of the sky. The World Meteorological Organization manual of codes includes a classification of particulates causing horizontal obscuration into categories of fog, ice fog, steam fog, mist, haze, smoke, volcanic ash, dust, sand, and snow. Sources for particles that cause haze include farming, traffic, industry, windy weather, volcanic activity and wildfires.\nSeen from afar and depending on the direction of view with respect to the Sun, haze may appear brownish or bluish, while mist tends to be bluish grey instead. Whereas haze often is considered a phenomenon occurring in dry air, mist formation is a phenomenon in saturated, humid air. However, haze particles may act as condensation nuclei that leads to the subsequent vapor condensation and formation of mist droplets; such forms of haze are known as \"wet haze\"."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d2/MosqueinAbuja.jpg/330px-MosqueinAbuja.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Haze","wikidata":"Q643546"},{"title":"Tangerine (color)","blocks":[{"text":"The color tangerine is a tone of orange named after the fruit of the same name."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2a/TangerineFruit.jpg/330px-TangerineFruit.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tangerine_(color)","wikidata":"Q846504"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4c/Tangerine_2009-03-11.jpg/500px-Tangerine_2009-03-11.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4c/Tangerine_2009-03-11.jpg/1280px-Tangerine_2009-03-11.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4c/Tangerine_2009-03-11.jpg/500px-Tangerine_2009-03-11.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ATangerine_2009-03-11.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/68/Pomological_Watercolor_POM00006444.jpg/250px-Pomological_Watercolor_POM00006444.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_con